{
 "complaint": {
  "complaint_ref": "260604-118372",
  "date_received": "2026-06-04",
  "product": "Money transfer, virtual currency, or money service",
  "sub_product": "Domestic (US) money transfer",
  "issue": "To be coded by the complaint team",
  "company": "Lakeshore Bank",
  "consumer_state": "MI",
  "submitted_via": "Web",
  "consumer_narrative": "On May 27, 2026 I found an electric bike for sale on a neighborhood buy/sell group for $650. The seller, who said his name was Tyler, told me another buyer was coming that evening and he would only hold the bike if I paid through Zelle first, because Zelle is 'instant and protected by your bank'. On May 28 at about 9 am I sent $650 from my Lakeshore checking account in the Lakeshore app to the e-mail address he gave me. After that he stopped answering and the listing disappeared. This was an unauthorized transaction as far as I'm concerned, because I never authorized this man to keep my money without giving me the bike. I called Lakeshore on May 29 and the representative said that since I sent it myself it counts as 'authorized' and there is nothing they can do. At the branch I asked them to do a chargeback like my credit card company would, and they told me Zelle payments don't have chargebacks. Then my car payment came out on June 2 and my account went negative because of this, and they hit me with a $35 overdraft fee on top of everything. I am asking Lakeshore Bank to reimburse the $650 to my checking account. Banks advertise Zelle as safe, so they should stand behind it."
 },
 "bank_records": {
  "zelle_payment": {
   "sent": "2026-05-28 09:07 ET",
   "amount": "$650.00",
   "recipient_token": "t.rangel.rides@example.com",
   "recipient_name_from_network": "TYLER RANGEL",
   "channel": "Lakeshore mobile app on the customer's registered phone (enrolled 2022-11-03), Face ID sign-in",
   "first_payment_to_recipient": true
  },
  "contact_history": [
   "2026-05-29 phone: customer reported paying for a bike that was never delivered. Logged as scam claim (customer-initiated payment), not as an unauthorized-transfer claim.",
   "2026-05-29: good-faith recall request sent to the recipient's bank.",
   "2026-06-01 branch visit: customer asked for a 'chargeback'; advised that Zelle payments are not card transactions.",
   "2026-06-05: recipient's bank replied that no funds remain in the account."
  ],
  "fees": "2026-06-02: ACH auto-loan debit of $412.18 posted; account overdrawn by $87.44; overdraft fee of $35.00 assessed."
 },
 "draft_company_response": "Dear Ms. Solberg, thank you for contacting us through the CFPB, and we are sorry to hear about your experience. Our review confirmed that the Zelle payment of $650.00 on May 28, 2026 was sent from your enrolled mobile device after Face ID sign-in, so it was authorized by you and does not qualify for reimbursement as an unauthorized transfer. Because the recipient did not pose as Lakeshore Bank, a government agency or Zelle, the payment also does not qualify under our imposter-scam reimbursement program. On May 29 we asked the recipient's bank to return the funds as a courtesy; unfortunately, it reported that no funds remained. Zelle payments are not card transactions and cannot be charged back through a card network. As a courtesy, we have refunded the $35.00 overdraft fee assessed on June 2, 2026; the credit will appear within two business days. We encourage you to report the seller at ReportFraud.ftc.gov and to the group where the bike was listed. Sincerely, Lakeshore Bank Customer Advocacy",
